{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested: R5 slice A (items 1-3) at exact head e113d9f4ff71d05308a49270f1c1f647d9ec83bd, tree de92e825383ed59d9a77a611b0d8cb24a6413aa7 (review request 0868e880, promise 8444390b, implementation 12fb37c9 / a42f84f6). There is one required documentation finding, F1. I found no code defect, and the code, tests, gate and controls pass my checks. A successor that changes only the two sentences in F1 needs no new gate (docs/testing.md:597), and its review is short.\n\nRead order: I read the primary, notes/implementation/2026-10-24-hosted-mcp-admission.md (artifact 3ce4fec8), first and in full. Then I read the whole diff from the branch base a138c30c: hosted-tools.ts, hosted.ts, index.ts, service.ts, worker.ts, the entry-point script and seals, the tests, docs/mcp.md, docs/security/entry-points.md and notes/2026-10-13-hosted-mcp-endpoint.md. I also read catalogue.ts (bindingsOf, whoOf, listed, selected) for the admission predicates. The branch starts at 6c15e85f and merges a138c30c (2a5de433). Merging the head into Main de590030 gives exactly its tree, de92e825. Its 19 changed paths are the 19 artifacts.\n\nF1 (required, documentation). Two sentences in maintained documents are false at this head, and would stay false on Main:\n- docs/mcp.md:1772 says \"the local slice A implementation and its pending verification are recorded in the admission note\". The verification has closed: gate 58335 passed at this head.\n- notes/2026-10-13-hosted-mcp-endpoint.md (the new R5 paragraph) says \"Its compiler, runtime, seal, gate, and deployment results are not yet claimed\". The compiler, runtime, seal and gate results are now claimed and pass. Only deployment is not claimed.\nThis is the same class as F1 in my kernel review 07f5b878: the status convention (sweep 25323213) asks for status that matches Main once the head lands. Requested fix: say that the admission note records the validation and the passing gate, and that deployment is not claimed. \"The explicit deployment choice remains pending\" is true at landing, and the planner updates it with the deploy. The dated implementation note may keep its own run-by-run wording.\n\nRequest items, against parent R5 8a856f92 items 1-3 and request 12fb37c9:\n1. Opt-in. mcpRoute answers 404 unless hostedMcpTools(env) is non-null and both namespaces are bound. That check comes before any body read or namespace addressing. hostedMcpTools returns null unless MCP_ENABLED is exactly \"true\". MCP_TOOL_ADDITIONS must be a string of at most 1,024 characters, parse as an array of distinct known names, and hold at most 18 of them. Every malformed form closes the endpoint. hosted-opt-in.test.ts traps idFromName, idFromString, get and the request body. It checks the five closed settings on /mcp and /mcp/agents, the missing-namespace cases, and that a valid opt-in reaches 401.\n2. Narrow default. HOSTED_TOOL_OMISSIONS is exactly the eight names in item 2. An addition restores only the names it lists. The policy controls both listing (snapshot) and dispatch (#genericAdmitted in call). Omitting genericTools keeps the local defaults, including custom act and actions aliases in default mode. The hosted.scope witness runs the real path: worker.fetch, then McpAgent with Env settings, then HostedAgent, then McpService. It lists and calls each omitted tool and gets unknown-tool, and room still answers.\n3. Hidden tools refuse by name. A named call takes a fresh snapshot on every attempt and needs selected and listed under fresh standing. In #perform, after the descriptor and digest checks, it needs an exact current binding: name, definition, kind, declaration and digest, selected and listed. #admitTarget then narrows the inventory to the actual target alone, so a grant on scope A does not admit scope B. Generic act, prepare with a kind, view actions and install_application (on the exact Rules target) share that fence. All of this runs before custody, signing or package fetch. The witnesses trap custody, signing and package fetch. They use a second membership that lacks the grant, a standing withdrawn after listing, a --tools exclusion and a code-granted join that must still pass.\nAuthority comes only from fresh state. A cached descriptor is kept only when the configuration digest matches and the snapshot was not invalidated. It decides only which error is reported (stale-descriptor before unknown-tool), never whether a call is admitted. Unknown standing and code-granted acts keep their earlier admission, and the native scope still judges every admitted act, as the note says.\n\nEvidence:\n- Gate. Request 0868e880 points to the manifest at /private/tmp/artroom-security-r5a-review-evidence-proposal-source-20261024-v2/manifest.json, SHA-256 44a0c12d, 37,867 bytes. All 92 inputs match their recorded SHA-256 (36,405,326 bytes). Gate 58335's result.json (SHA-256 0785e6af) records expectedHead e113d9f4 and expectedTree de92e825, with unchanged checkpoints before and after. So the gate ran at exactly this head, as planner ruling 58f58662 requires. The raw stages/test.log reads \"Test Files 495 passed | 1 skipped (496)\" and \"Tests 3526 passed | 3 skipped (3529)\". The Node blocks report 39 of 39 and 55 of 55. The four new witnesses pass at lines 2368-2370 and 458261, and binding.scope at line 455099. The failed gate at c0208a87 (3,524 passed, 2 failed, 3 skipped) and the failed continuation at 04a47985 stay recorded as failed.\n- Controls. Each control's log shows the named red: the target control reached custody and accepted an ungranted target (1 passed, 2 failed); the alias control returned an error for a valid alias (2 passed, 1 failed); the endpoint control answered 401 instead of 404; the forwarding control listed create (16 passed, 1 failed). The endpoint and forwarding controls ran on bytes identical to the head's worker.ts and hosted.ts. The target and alias controls ran on the service.ts before the gate repair, which differs from the head in 23 lines: the cached-identity selection, the catalogue check, and moving #admitTarget after the stale checks with the mounted-card view fence. Those repair lines have no mutation control of their own. Their natural reds are the two gate failures at c0208a87 and the binding-case failure at 04a47985. By my reading, call() already refuses the authority cases I could construct, so the catalogue check is a second fence for naming, not the only authority check.\n\nDocument checks at e113d9f4:\n- git diff --check from a138c30c is clean;\n- check-commit-messages from a138c30c reports 11 checked, 0 flagged; the longest subject is 70 characters;\n- section citations on an export report 1,877 resolved, 0 broken and 5 known unresolved. The script is identical to Main's.\n\nArchitecture: the policy is one small module that both the Worker route and the agent constructor read. The service applies it to listing and dispatch from one predicate. Target admission reuses the catalogue's own predicates (bindingsOf, selected, listed) on a one-scope inventory, so the list and the call cannot disagree on who may act. The cost is fresh reads on every named attempt: four GETs for issue.comment and five for issue.create in the budget fixture, with exact routes checked. The 70 refused resubmissions now make 4 reads and 1 post each. Other tool bounds are unchanged.\nSecurity: I found no defect. The endpoint fails closed on a missing or malformed setting before it reads a body or addresses an object. The narrow default removes the creating, joining, credential and raw-read tools from hosted agents unless an operator adds them by name. A hidden or ungranted call refuses before custody, signing, the act POST or a package fetch, and a remembered name keeps no expired grant. The seal values change for worker.ts and hosted.ts, and four new owners are sealed (hosted-tools.ts, service.ts, catalogue.ts, inventory.ts); the entry-point Node block passed in the gate. The inventory now models the real policy with valid opt-in, and a disabled-settings control removes only the /mcp rows. Unknown standing on a target outside the configured memberships is still admitted at the client, and the native scope judges it. This is unchanged and stated.\nSimplification: about 60 lines of service change and a 16-line policy module, with no compatibility shim. Nonblocking:\n- the generic act and prepare refusal names the kind (\"No tool is named publish in this list\"), which reads oddly for a generic tool;\n- after a native key revocation, a hosted agent now gets unknown-tool instead of the scope's refusal reason. That is what item 3 asks for. The agent can no longer see why, though, and a sentence in docs/mcp.md would help.\n\nResidual risk for the planner's deploy, not a defect of this head:\n- After this head lands, every deploy of a Worker built from Main closes hosted MCP on that Worker unless MCP_ENABLED is \"true\" there. That includes deploys made for other changes, such as the next archive repair to dap-grow. MCP_ENABLED is a new variable that no Worker has yet. The deploy keeps existing variables (--keep-vars) but does not add this one, so the planner must set it, and any additions, in the same step as the first such deploy of each Worker, as decision 8904bf5b plans. In the repository configs, three Workers bind both MCP namespaces: artroom-scope (wrangler.jsonc), dap-grow (wrangler.grow.jsonc) and dap-demo (wrangler.demo.jsonc). dap-lending binds neither, so its endpoint is already closed.\n- A malformed MCP_TOOL_ADDITIONS, for example a typo, also closes the endpoint.\nNo deployment or hosted claim is made or approved here.\n\nFor approval of the successor, I need a tree that differs from e113d9f4 only in the two F1 sentences. Nothing else here needs to be redone.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:3ce4fec8f6b3716a59613be2141783f86f9797db","binding":"assigned","head":"e113d9f4ff71d05308a49270f1c1f647d9ec83bd","head_news_acknow